/*****************************************************************************
    kantLoop -- LOOP CLOSURE, STEP 1: DOES THE TRAMPOLINE DISPATCH?
    2026-08-11, SEQ 52.

    THE ONE QUESTION. parse() forks on rStuff.parseMethod, which is a C++
    function pointer. parseViaKant stands in that slot and forwards to a kant
    action. Does a rule invoked from incant actually reach kant code?

    WHY DISPATCH IS PROVEN BEFORE ANY SHIM EXISTS, and this is the whole
    reason the fixture is this small. The support library -- lit, parseR,
    leaveRule -- is not callable from kant yet. If the first kant parse method
    tried to match input, a red would be ambiguous between "the trampoline does
    not dispatch" and "the shims are missing", and disambiguating it afterwards
    costs more than sequencing it costs now. So this rule's kant method
    CONSUMES NOTHING and only records that it ran. Matching comes next.

    ⚠ SO THIS FIXTURE DOES NOT ASSERT A CURSOR, AND THAT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT
    OF THE STANDING RULE. Asserting cursor position rather than verdict is
    ruled for PARSE fixtures, and this one deliberately does not parse: there
    is nothing for a cursor to be right or wrong about until a term can match.
    The cursor obligation lands on the fixture that follows this one, and
    naming it here is what stops a later reader taking this file's shape as
    the precedent.

    THE COUNTER IS THE INSTRUMENT (H4, presence-with-value). klFired is
    printed as a scalar before and after, so "it dispatched" is a number that
    moved and not the absence of an error line. A run where the trampoline
    silently did nothing prints 0 twice and says so.

    ⚠ NEGATIVE CONTROL, IN-FILE (H7). ScafU is defined identically and bound
    the same way, but NO kpScafU action exists. It must refuse BY NAME on
    stderr and leave its counter at zero. Without it, a green above cannot
    tell "the trampoline found my action" from "the trampoline runs anything
    it is handed" -- and the refusal path is the one that would rot silently,
    because nothing else exercises it.
*****************************************************************************/
Start();

registry(cOMMANDs);
define parseMethod immediateAction=parseRuleMethod noPrint; ;
registry(cOMMANDs);
define parseTerms immediateAction=parseTermCount noPrint; ;
registry(cOMMANDs);
define traceParse immediateAction=traceParse; ;

/*  THE KANT PARSE METHOD. Named by the trampoline's convention: rule ScafK is
    served by action kpScafK. It consumes nothing -- see the header.  */
register(KantLoop);
define
    klFired = 0;
    klFiredU = 0;
    kpScafK code={
        klFired = klFired + 1;
        };
    ;

/*  THE RULES. ScafK has a kant method; ScafU deliberately has none.  */
register(Ladder);
define
    ScafK isRule "k"- parseTerms=1 parseMethod=parseViaKant;
    ScafU isRule "u"- parseTerms=1 parseMethod=parseViaKant;
    ;

search reset stack Grokking KantLoop Ladder list;

traceParse('on');

print "KL before      : klFired =" klFired:;
ScafK('k');
print "KL after ScafK : klFired =" klFired " (want 1 -- 0 means the trampoline never dispatched)":;

print "KL control     : ScafU has NO kpScafU action; a refusal line must follow":;
ScafU('u');
print "KL after ScafU : klFiredU =" klFiredU " (want 0, and the refusal named kpScafU by name)":;

print "KANTLOOP SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
stop();
